.TH DEV 9
.SH NAME
dev \- writing a kernel device server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:vfs"
.PP
.nf
.B "Handler :: #type proc(server: rawptr, s: ^Session, tag: Tag,"
.B "    request: ^Msg, reply: ^Msg, buf: []u8)"
.B "Abort   :: proc(server: rawptr, tag: vectra9.Tag)"
.PP
.B "server_init  :: proc(sv: ^Server, name: string, handler: Handler, state: rawptr) -> Error"
.B "server_start :: proc(sv: ^Server, workers: int, payload: int, abort: Abort) -> bool"
.B "register_device :: proc(sv: ^Server) -> bool"
.B "mount_device :: proc(ns: ^Namespace, spec: string, target_path: string) -> Errno"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A kernel device is a
.B Server
(see
.IR vfs (9))
whose handler answers out of a table it owns rather than out of a mounted
tree.
It registers under a one-letter name and is reached at
.BI # letter
before it is bound anywhere.
.IR cons (3)
is the worked example below.
It serves
.BR /dev :
.BR cons ,
.BR consctl ,
.BR null ,
.BR zero ,
.BR fb ,
.BR fbctl ,
.B scancode
and
.BR eia0 .
.SS The node table
A device's files are rows in a fixed array, not a mounted subtree.
.I Devfs
keeps a
.B Dev_Node
per file:
a name, a parent index and a
.B Dev_Kind
that says what the file
.I does
rather than what it contains.
.B Dev_Kind
is a separate enum from the node so that a new file is a row in the table
and a case in the two switches below,
and nothing about walking, listing or the fid table has to learn a new
file kind.
The table is
.B .rodata
and needs no lock.
A qid's path is the node's index plus one, so a zeroed qid never names the
root by accident.
.SS The two switches
.I Devfs_read
and
.I devfs_write
each switch on the node's
.B Dev_Kind
once the fid has been resolved to a node index.
Everything a file
.I does
lives in one arm of one of these two switches:
.B null
discards writes and reads at end of file,
.B zero
discards writes and reads as an endless run of zero bytes,
.B fb
reads and writes the framebuffer's bytes at an offset,
.B cons
and the taps park.
Adding a device kind that neither streams nor parks is one row in the
node table and one case in each switch.
.SS The fid table and its lock
A device server shares
.IR vfs (9)'s
.B Fid_Table
the way any server does, and guards it the same way
.I devfs
does: one spinlock, held only around a fid lookup, never across a park or
a write to a framebuffer.
.B Dev_Tree.lock
in
.I devfs
covers exactly
.IR fidtab_bind ,
.I fidtab_node
and
.I fidtab_release
and nothing else.
A handler that took a spinlock and then called
.I sync.sleep
or wrote through a framebuffer pointer would stop every other request on
the server, and on a single core, the machine.
Counting how many fids hold a file whose open state means something
(a
.B ctl
file, a tap) is done under this same lock, at the moment the fid table
changes, so the count and the open flag can never drift apart.
What the count causes, reverting a mode or releasing a tap, happens
outside the lock, because that may itself take a lock of the thing being
reverted.
.SS A read that parks
A device whose bytes are not there yet, such as
.BR /dev/cons ,
parks its reader on a rendezvous instead of returning early.
The loop is:
.IP
.EX
for {
	if vfs.server_flushed(&t.server, tag) {
		reply^ = vectra9.error_reply(vectra9.EINTR)
		return
	}
	if n := take(source, buf[:room]); n > 0 {
		reply^ = vectra9.Rread{data = buf[:n]}
		return
	}
	sync.sleep(&source.ready, ready_condition, w)
}
.EE
.PP
The flush is checked
.I before
the drain, on every pass.
A request the transport is about to abandon has already had its tag
reclaimed, and a reply built for it is dropped.
Draining first would consume the bytes into that dropped reply, and the
next reader would find nothing where a byte had in fact arrived.
Checking the flush first leaves the bytes for the read that comes after.
.I Sync.sleep
loops rather than returning once, because a wake is a hint: one byte can
wake two parked readers and only one of them will find it, so the other
has to test its condition again and park again.
.SS The abort hook
.I Server_start
takes an
.B Abort
hook, called by
.IR mnt (9)
when a
.B Tflush
names a request in flight.
A device with parked readers wakes every one of them, not only the one
named by the flushed tag: what identifies a parked reader is the
rendezvous it waits on, not its tag, and re-testing a condition that was
not flushed is cheaper than keeping a rendezvous per tag.
Each woken reader re-checks
.I vfs.server_flushed
for its own tag.
The one that was flushed answers EINTR, and the others
park again.
The flushed bit lives on the request slot in
.IR mnt (9)
and is authoritative: a device's own copy of it would have to be cleared
at the moment the slot is reused, which is a moment the device cannot
see.
.SS The ctl convention
A file named
.B ctl
(or, as with the console,
.BR consctl )
is how a device takes an operation 9P has no message for.
Four rules apply to every
.B ctl
file in the tree:
.TP 4
1.
One write is one command.
Nothing is buffered between writes, and a command may not be split
across two of them.
.TP 4
2.
A command nothing recognises is
.BR EINVAL ,
and the write takes none of it.
Silence would let a caller believe it changed something.
.TP 4
3.
The file reads back as the writes that would restore its current
state, not a status report in a different vocabulary.
A caller may save what it reads and write it back later.
.TP 4
4.
The last close reverts it.
.PD
.PP
.I Consctl_close
puts the console back into cooked, echoing mode when the last fid
holding
.B /dev/consctl
is clunked, so a program that turns raw mode on and then faults does not
leave a console nobody can type at.
.SS Workers
.I Server_start
is given a worker count.
.I Devfs
uses
.BR "mnt.MAX_REQUESTS + 1" :
one worker for every request slot the transport can carry at once, so
every parked read gets its own thread and never waits behind another
read that is itself parked, and one more to serve a
.B Tflush
that unsticks one, since a flush never parks.
.SS Streams, offset and creation
A stream file (a console, a tap, a
.B ctl
file) reports size zero from
.B Tgetattr
and ignores the offset in
.B Tread
and
.BR Twrite ,
because a second read at offset zero does not read the same bytes
again.
A file backed by real storage, such as
.BR /dev/fb ,
reports its true size and honours the offset.
Creating, removing, renaming or linking anything in a device directory is
.BR EPERM :
the shape of the tree is the driver table, and a caller does not get to
add a row to it.
.SS Registering and binding
A device calls
.I vfs.server_init
with its handler and a one-letter name, then either
.I vfs.server_start
to move onto
.IR mnt (9)
with workers, or nothing, to stay on the synchronous transport.
It then calls
.I vfs.register_device
to publish itself at
.BI # letter
in the fixed, boot-time device table, and
.I vfs.mount_device
to bind that tree at a path in a namespace.
Order matters:
.I server_start
must run before
.I register_device
so that nothing can reach the server on a stack it does not yet have,
and any producer thread that feeds the device (a keyboard poller, a
serial line) starts last, so it never fills a ring nobody is yet parked
to drain.
Duplicate one-letter names are refused rather than shadowed.
.SS Synchronous devices and devices with workers
.IR env (3)
and
.IR srv (3)
never call
.IR server_start .
Every message either answers a table lookup or, in
.BR srv 's
case, attaches to a server found in the table.
Nothing in either
waits, so there is nothing for a worker thread to do while a caller
blocks and nothing for a
.B Tflush
to abandon.
Their whole handler runs under one spinlock, the way
.I vfs.static_handler
does.
.IR cons (3)
calls
.I server_start
because a read of
.B /dev/cons
can wait indefinitely for a key, and a caller with a deadline needs a
request it can give up on.
.SS Resolver hooks
A synchronous device that must answer on behalf of the calling process
registers a resolver hook rather than importing
.IR user (9)
directly.
.IR srv (3)
registers
.B Fd_Resolver
and
.IR env (3)
registers
.BR Group_Resolver .
Both are supplied by
.IR user (9)
at syscall setup, because it alone owns descriptor tables
and environment groups.
A resolver runs under the device's own spinlock, on the calling thread,
because the transport is synchronous and "the current process" is
therefore well defined.
A resolver may look up tables of its own and may nest a spinlock, but
may not send a message or take a lock that can park.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI # letter
a registered device, reachable outside every namespace until it is
bound
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/devfs
.br
